WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.100
MARTIN: Hi, Mete.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.350
METE: Hi, Martin,
what's going on?

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.010
MARTIN: I loaded some e-commerce
order data into BigQuery.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.930
Now I want to
transform that data

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.540
and create a new table that's
optimized for my business

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.430
intelligence dashboard.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.800
Somebody told me you might have
pointers for how to do that.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.170
METE: Yes, you
should check out DBT.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.050
It's a third party tool to
transform data by simply

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.120
writing select statements.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.730
It doesn't extract
or load data but it

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.140
is extremely good
at transforming

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.049
data that's already loaded
into your warehouse.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:32.980
MARTIN: Cool!

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.157
Will do.

00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:40.001
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.860
Hi, Mete.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.780
Thanks for your tip about DBT.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.750
I found a really nice tutorial
on how to transform data

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.400
within BigQuery.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.930
It does pretty much what I need.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.480
It joins a customer
and an order table

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.820
into a new dashboard
friendly table.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.750
I got it working on my Google
Cloud account, can I show you?

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.700
METE: Sure, let's see.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.820
MARTIN: All right, I'll
switch over here to this.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.590
I'll go into the jaffle
shop example that I loaded.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.135
I'm running DBT Run.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:20.825
We'll see if it runs.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.300
So there, it says
completed successfully.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:36.720
And if I go over to my
project and I go into BigQuery

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.340
and I go into my
customers' data set here.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.060
I have a table and
a customer table

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.660
that was created by this job.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.050
I click Preview and
sure enough, there's

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.530
my denormalized customer data.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.850
METE: Good job, Martin.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.530
But I have one question.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.290
How are you going to run
this in the long run?

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.890
I can imagine typing DBT
Run every day will get old

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.880
pretty quickly.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.910
Also, you probably
want BigQuery to always

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:10.840
have the latest data even
when you're out on vacation.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.830
MARTIN: OK.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.100
Well, I guess I could trigger
the command on a schedule

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.250
from a cron job on my laptop.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.080
METE: Wouldn't your laptop be
closed when you're on vacation?

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.120
MARTIN: Maybe I could
get a virtual machine

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.270
to run this in the Cloud.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.420
METE: You could, but why keep
a virtual machine running 24/7

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.660
when you only need for
a few seconds per day?

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.210
And you probably don't want to
add server maintenance to your

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.810
to do list.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.990
MARTIN: Ugh, I hate
managing servers.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.940
I'm not good at it.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.950
METE: Let's go
serverless instead.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.140
MARTIN: But how would I
run a custom command line

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.200
tool from a serverless
product like Cloud Functions?

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.340
The Cloud Functions runtime
doesn't have DBT installed.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.280
METE: That's true, but
we have other options.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.660
In Cloud Run you can create your
own runtime with a container.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.210
There's a base container
image with DBT,

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.840
we can start with that.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.030
MARTIN: That sounds great.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.020
How do I set that up?

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.080
METE: Open your Google Cloud
project and I'll show you.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.910
MARTIN: All right, so
I'm here in my project.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.980
METE: Cloud Run lets
you run containers

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.080
without having to
maintain a server

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.560
and you only pay
when your code runs.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.280
MARTIN: Cool!

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.030
But I don't know much about
containers or .go As a matter

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.460
of fact, I don't even have .go
installed here on my laptop.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.910
METE: No worries, we'll
build the container

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.270
using Cloud Build.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.140
Your code in Cloud Run is
triggered by HTTP requests.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.170
In this case, we will
run the DBT command

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:47.370
in a shell script in
response to an HTTP request.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:52.260
Let's find a simple HTTP server
that can run shell scripts.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.200
Go to Cloud Run tutorial
and find a sample

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.660
for running a shell
script in Cloud Run

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.210
and copy that code
to your machine.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.950
MARTIN: OK, so I'm
in the quick start

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.830
for build and
deploy on Cloud Run.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:10.750
Scrolling down the page here,
I want to run a shell script.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.720
Here's a shell script.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.089
OK, it seems like there
is a Go program here

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:17.709
that can run shell scripts.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:23.320
So I copy that, I paste
that into my machine.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.710
OK, I've saved it as
invoke.go on my machine.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:28.910
METE: Cool.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.260
Then save your comment
in a script file.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.010
MARTIN: All right.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.000
I did that already.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.760
I have a script.sh
here on my machine.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.070
METE: Now let's
containerize our code.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:43.670
I sent you a .go file
for the container image.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:48.140
We are relying on a base image
that includes the DBT tool.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.040
We are also copying the go HTTP
server along with the script

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.150
file to the container.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.940
The entry point to the
container is the HTTP server.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.150
Let's build the container
with Cloud Build,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.000
I sent you the command
for doing that.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:03.950
MARTIN: All right.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:08.760
You sent me, let's
see, build container.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.400
So I'm running that now.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.320
All right, it finished
building the container.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.500
METE: Let's deploy the
container to Cloud Run.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.720
That's the second
command I have sent you.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:20.470
MARTIN: All right.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.490
I got it.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.620
So deploy container, very good.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.020
I'm running that now.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.070
All right.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.810
The script for deploying
container finished running.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:33.940
So it says something about
serving traffic 100% at URL

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.730
and there's a URL with a
strange hash code in it.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.640
What's this URL?

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.290
METE: That's the service URL.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.740
When you hit that URL, the
code you just uploaded runs.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:45.940
Try it!

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:46.780
MARTIN: Oh, cool.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:47.530
All right.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:52.390
So I'm going to my browser here,
pasting it in, and running it.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.510
Let's see what happens.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.350
Oh, cool.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:06:00.210
So I see the printout that I
previously got from my DBT job

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.330
in my console, I now see
that in my web browser.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:10.760
And let's see, if I go over
to BigQuery and refresh here.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.140
I open my customers data set,
click the customer table,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.240
and let's see.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.300
There is a table
there, I click Preview.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.240
And sure enough, there's my
denormalized customer table,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.330
including order
dates and everything.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:26.840
METE: Nice.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.560
MARTIN: Great.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.130
All right.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:34.380
So let me delete this
table again to make sure

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.230
that we can recreate it.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:41.820
Customers, delete.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.060
So I moved my DBT job from
my laptop to the Cloud.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.110
I can run the DBT
job by hitting URL,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.710
but I still have to manual
hit that URL from my browser.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.980
The job won't run by itself
when I'm on vacation.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.350
METE: That's right,
but we can fix that.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.215
Cloud Scheduler is a service
that can trigger an end

00:07:01.215 --> 00:07:03.830
point on a given schedule.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:08.240
Let's go to Cloud Scheduler
page and create a new job.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.090
MARTIN: All right, I'm
clicking Cloud Scheduler here.

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:23.200
Create new job, and, OK, so I
have a bunch of fields here.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.900
I guess for a name I'll say,
right, DBT job, perhaps.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.200
Frequency, this is something
with stars and numbers.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.240
What's this about Mete?

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.970
METE: So that's the frequency
that the Cloud Scheduler

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:38.560
will call your service.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.790
It's in cron format.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.650
So I guess in this case, we want
to run it once a day every day

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:45.680
in the afternoon.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:52.240
So we will enter something
like 016, star, star.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.350
MARTIN: Very good, I did that.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.880
And let's see, time zone.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.595
I want it to be in my time zone.

00:08:00.595 --> 00:08:03.790
I'm in the Pacific, US
Pacific Standard Time.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:11.660
Oh and I needed to
fix the name here.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.670
All right, so Mete,
the next one is target.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.170
So I can choose between HTTP,
pub sub, and App Engine HTTP.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.470
What should I pick here?

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:23.100
METE: So in this case, we
want to hit a Cloud Run

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.185
service, which is an HTTP end
point, so we'll choose HTTP.

00:08:27.185 --> 00:08:27.810
MARTIN: Oh, OK.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:28.740
HTTP it is.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.370
And for URL, I guess I
should paste in that URL

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.409
that I hit manually before?

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:37.679
METE: Yes, that's the
Cloud Run service URL.

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:38.520
MARTIN: Excellent.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.809
And if I remember correctly I
hit it with a git, with a post.

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:44.370
I'm clicking Create here.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:52.010
And look, there's my new job.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.830
METE: Yes, it's created
but it's not running yet.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.470
We can actually test it out
by just clicking the run now

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.870
button to trigger it manually.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.040
MARTIN: Oh, cool.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.794
OK, I'm clicking Run now.

00:09:03.794 --> 00:09:05.320
Uh-huh, very good.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.520
Something happened.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:12.300
All right, so I'm going into
my project here in BigQuery.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.870
I see the customers
data set there

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.450
and there's the customers
table that I deleted before.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.830
It has been recreated.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.740
Let's just have a look at
the preview of that table.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.040
And yep, there is my
denormalized order data.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.020
Table is there,
everything is working.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:31.080
But Mete, I'm accessing this
from an incognito window.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.900
I just hit the URL here
and it just came through.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:37.590
Can anyone access that
URL and run my job?

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:40.860
METE: That's right, it's
a public service right now

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.340
but we can make it private so
only authenticated users can

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.020
access it.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.170
Then we can configure
class schedules

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:52.010
to use a service account to
access this private service.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.840
I have written a
tutorial that explains

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.770
how to do that, the link is
in the description below.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.530
MARTIN: OK, so let's
summarize what we did.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.420
We started with
Cloud Scheduler that

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:07.070
hit a URL on a schedule that
triggered a Cloud Run job.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:11.130
That Cloud Run job ran a script
file, a shell script file,

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:16.130
which in turn is running a
DBT command that updates,

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.250
transforms the data in my
BigQuery data warehouse.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.720
METE: Exactly!

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:25.650
And the key point is that we
run DBT commands without having

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:29.220
to maintain servers and
we run it on a schedule

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.967
so that you don't need to be
around to trigger it every day.

00:10:32.967 --> 00:10:34.800
MARTIN: I like that I
don't have to remember

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.657
to run this every day manually.

00:10:37.657 --> 00:10:39.240
METE: Now you can
go back to the beach

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.090
without having to think
about service, Martin.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:42.990
[CHUCKLES]

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:46.040
[THEME MUSIC]

